Connect domains, composition and inverse questions (@math-function-structure: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Statements-quantifiers-and-counterexamples.lingua#r_096B0C0E0744E81B6D17247FEA") { r_07C292DB06749E12B30235383A
A function includes a domain and an assignment of one output to every allowed input. A formula is one representation; a table or a verbal rule may also define a function. The range is the set of outputs actually reached. Naming a larger possible output set does not establish that every value in it occurs.

Consider f(x) = 3x + 2 for 0 ≤ x ≤ 4, with dimensionless x. Its range is 2 ≤ y ≤ 14: the function is increasing, reaches both endpoints, and every intervening y is produced by x = (y − 2)/3. The inverse has domain [2, 14] and returns a value in [0, 4]. Square brackets include the endpoint. Evaluating the inverse formula at 20 is arithmetically possible but lies outside this inverse's stated domain.

Now let g(y) = y² for 2 ≤ y ≤ 8. The composition g(f(x)) means first apply f and then g. For it to be defined, f(x) must be in g's domain. Thus 2 ≤ 3x + 2 ≤ 8, giving 0 ≤ x ≤ 2. The result is (3x + 2)², but keeping only that formula and forgetting the restricted domain changes the function.

An inverse function requires distinct allowed inputs to have distinct outputs. The squaring function on [−3, 3] sends −2 and 2 to the same value, so a single-valued inverse cannot recover both. Restricting the domain to [0, 3] gives the nonnegative square root as inverse on [0, 9]. Choosing [−3, 0] gives the negative square root instead. State the chosen branch. The notation f⁻¹ for an inverse does not mean 1/f.

**Read:** [Algebra and Trigonometry 2e](../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/sources/Algebra-and-Trigonometry.lingua#r_7FKH7S2VNZT97STHJ10KB186YX), sections 3.1–3.2 and 3.4–3.7; complete the relevant function, composition and inverse exercises. Selected publisher passages from 3.1, 3.2 and 3.7 were inspected for this block; the larger reading assignment remains learner work.

**Work:** verify both compositions of f and its inverse on their proper domains. Change g's domain to [5, 11] and derive the new allowed x interval. Give two different domain choices for the same formula that answer different questions. Explain why saturation or rounding can make physical reconstruction ambiguous even when a forward calculation is defined.

**Check:** explain a relation, domain, range, composition and inverse in words before using symbolic notation. Connect a mathematical inverse with the separate question of whether an actual measurement carries enough information to identify an input.

**Continue:** [State what a mathematical claim says about its objects](Statements-quantifiers-and-counterexamples.lingua#r_096B0C0E0744E81B6D17247FEA).

